!deck: Math::Multivariable-Calculus
!type: Basic Math
!tags: Math Calculus Multivariable

!id: ZETR9P2sZY
!front: Why is \( \int_C f\,ds \) guaranteed to be a scalar, and how do you rewrite it as an ordinary integral over \( t \)?
!back:
\( f \) evaluated along the curve is a scalar, and \( ds \) is a scalar, so the integrand \( f\,ds \) is scalar \( \times \) scalar. Substituting \( ds = \lvert\mathbf{r}'(t)\rvert\,dt \) and evaluating \( f \) at the moving point \( \mathbf{r}(t) \),
\[ \int_C f\,ds = \int_a^b f(\mathbf{r}(t))\,\lvert\mathbf{r}'(t)\rvert\,dt, \]
an ordinary single-variable integral.
!extra: Example: the mass of a wire with linear density \( f \).
~~~

!id: 4cdPvzzajc
!front: Why is \( \int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} \) a scalar even though both \( \mathbf{F} \) and \( d\mathbf{r} \) are vectors, and how do you rewrite it as an ordinary integral over \( t \)?
!back:
The dot product collapses the two vectors into a scalar before integration. Substituting \( d\mathbf{r} = \mathbf{r}'(t)\,dt \),
\[ \int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = \int_a^b \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{r}(t)) \cdot \mathbf{r}'(t)\,dt, \]
an ordinary single-variable integral of a scalar function of \( t \).
!extra: This is the work done by force field \( \mathbf{F} \) along \( C \).
~~~

!id: f3VzWNfKD1
!front: How does the work integral \( \int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} \) relate to the scalar line integral of a component of \( \mathbf{F} \)?
!back:
Using \( d\mathbf{r} = \mathbf{T}\,ds \),
\[ \int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = \int_C (\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{T})\,ds, \]
so the work integral is exactly the scalar line integral of the tangential component \( \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{T} \) — a scalar field — along \( C \).
!extra: In components, with \( \mathbf{F} = (P,Q,R) \), this is often written \( \int_C P\,dx + Q\,dy + R\,dz \), the dot product \( \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} \) spelled out term by term.
~~~

!id: 2yie7MA2jC
!front: Why is \( \iint_S f\,dS \) a scalar, and how do you rewrite it as an ordinary double integral?
!back:
\( f \) is a scalar and \( dS \) is a scalar, so the integrand is scalar \( \times \) scalar. Substituting \( dS = \lvert\mathbf{r}_u \times \mathbf{r}_v\rvert\,du\,dv \),
\[ \iint_S f\,dS = \iint_D f(\mathbf{r}(u,v))\,\lvert\mathbf{r}_u \times \mathbf{r}_v\rvert\,du\,dv, \]
an ordinary double integral over the flat parameter region \( D \).
!extra: Example: the mass of a sheet with surface density \( f \).
~~~

!id: FOaFE5o6SW
!front: Why is the flux integral \( \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S} \) a scalar, and how do you rewrite it as an ordinary double integral?
!back:
The dot product of the vector field \( \mathbf{F} \) with the vector area element \( d\mathbf{S} \) collapses to a scalar. Substituting \( d\mathbf{S} = (\mathbf{r}_u \times \mathbf{r}_v)\,du\,dv \),
\[ \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S} = \iint_D \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \cdot (\mathbf{r}_u \times \mathbf{r}_v)\,du\,dv, \]
an ordinary double integral of a scalar function of \( (u,v) \).
!extra: The flux measures how much of \( \mathbf{F} \) passes through \( S \).
~~~

!id: rXeYXBPPt5
!front: How does the flux integral \( \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S} \) relate to the scalar surface integral of a component of \( \mathbf{F} \)?
!back:
Using \( d\mathbf{S} = \mathbf{n}\,dS \),
\[ \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S} = \iint_S (\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n})\,dS, \]
so flux is exactly the scalar surface integral of the normal component \( \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} \) of the field.
~~~

!id: 1mtnlIwet8
!front: Why does \( \iiint_V f\,dV \) have no vector-valued analogue, unlike the line and surface integrals?
!back:
A solid region in \( \mathbb{R}^3 \) has no leftover direction to carry: after using all three dimensions to define the region, there is no tangent or normal structure left for a "vector volume element" to point along. So \( dV \) is intrinsically scalar, and
\[ \iiint_V f\,dV = \iiint_V f\,dx\,dy\,dz \]
is just three nested ordinary integrals.
~~~

!id: fxY1Jf3lEd
!front: What single pattern do all five vector-calculus integrals share in regards to the result type, and what role does the dot product play in it?
!back:
Every one of them evaluates to a scalar.

- When the field is vector-valued, the dot product with a vector differential element (\( d\mathbf{r} \) or \( d\mathbf{S} \)) is exactly what reduces the vector integrand to a scalar before integrating;
- when the field is already scalar, it's simply paired with a scalar element.

Seeing a "\( \cdot \)" next to a differential is the signal that two vectors are entering and one scalar is leaving.
~~~
